{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey rent control framework","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rent control and rent leveling exist only where a municipality adopts an ordinance; there is no statewide rent cap.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Coverage set by municipal rent control ordinances."},"exemptions":"HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized developments and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction is completed.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs for 30 years following completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and new lease requirement","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent, with written notice. Increases must not be unconscionable and must follow municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment. The landlord has to offer the tenant a new lease with the increased rent once the old lease has been terminated.","interaction":"State law requires rent increases to comply with municipal rent control ordinances where they exist.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in the converted building, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rent increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are allowed.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"No residential landlord may evict a tenant or fail to renew a lease without good cause; the landlord must prove the grounds in court and serve a Notice to Quit for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence; written or oral leases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit transfer and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and is responsible for its investment, notices and return. Willfully withholding the deposit of a tenant receiving government financial assistance carries a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500–$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceedings; unlawfully using deposit monies is a disorderly person offense punishable by a fine of at least $200, up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source of lawful income protection","requirement":"The Law Against Discrimination bars landlords and rental agencies from refusing to rent or discriminating in housing based on a prospective tenant's source of lawful income, among other protected classes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex residences as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings and owner-occupied single homes with rooms are exempt."},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (as to familial status).","penalty":"Monetary damages, an order to rent the unit to the complainant, and fines of up to $10,000 for a first offense under the LAD.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
